  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 320 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nCurrently, zonal flow aloft in the wake of a weak S/WV ridge and\na developing upper level trough over the Pacific Northwest.\nClosed upper level low over central Canada with an associated\nsfc trough/frontal boundary stretched from south central Canada\nsouth/southwest to across the Northern High Plains of MT/WY to\nour west. This surface pattern results in a south/southwesterly\nlow/mid level flow across the Dakotas, allowing for very dry \nair to advect into much of western and central North Dakota \nthis afternoon. A Red Flag Warning remains in effect for several\nmore hours for areas south and west of the Missouri River, where\nlatest observations report percent RH values as low as the \nlower to mid teens to lower 20s and steady southwest winds\nsustained to at least 15 knots (See Fire Weather Discussion\nBelow).\n\nUpper low over central Canada continues east tonight, and will\ndrive the aforementioned sfc trough south across western and\ncentral North Dakota late tonight through Friday AM. Winds will\nshift from south/southwesterly to northerly behind the FROPA,\nand will become gusty during the day Friday with a decent\npressure gradient and mixing persisting. Fri aft temperatures \ndrop 10-20 degrees from today, with a mix of sun and clouds. Zonal\nflow transitions to a west/southwesterly flow aloft tonight/Fri,\nwith an embedded wave bringing some light shower chances to\nnorthwest areas of the state during the day.\n\nWe remain in a quasi-zonal flow regime through Sat night, along\nwith the upper level jet stream sagging farther to the south.\nThis results in temperatures trending cooler/well below normal \nfor the weekend, with highs in the 60s both Saturday and Sunday\nand lows well into the 40s (30s are a safe bet for lower lying\nareas). Fairly potent upper low develops into the Pacific \nNorthwest Sat/Sat night, which further develops into the \nNorthern Rockies Sunday before lifting east-northeast across \nthe Northern Plains Sunday night through Monday. Increasing \nupper level divergence ahead of this wave, coupled with an \ninverted sfc trough moving from west to east across the region. \nThis feature will bring elevated widespread precipitation \nchances to our area, with forecast QPF ranging from 0.5 - 1 inch\nSun evening through Monday morning. Instability limited during \nthis period, so perhaps some embedded thunder though with \nchances for any severe low. \n\nThereafter, much of next week is favored to be in a\nwest/northwest flow pattern, which would support a gradual\nmoderation/warming of temperatures and minimal chances for\nprecipitation.",
